# Commands to run (from build directory, e.g. /app/build)

1. Compile the test into .class files
mkdir -p java-tests
javac -cp mdv_bindings/mdv/java/mdv4j.jar ../mdv_bindings/mdv/java/tests/CrudTest.java -d java-tests
This produces bytecode files such as mdv_bindings/mdv/java/tests/CrudTest.java → java-tests/CrudTest.class

2. Run the compiled class directly (recommended)
java -Djava.library.path=mdv_bindings/mdv/java -cp java-tests:mdv_bindings/mdv/java/mdv4j.jar CrudTest

where:

-Djava.library.path points to the directory containing the native JNI library (e.g., libmdv4j.so)
-cp includes the compiled classes directory and the SWIG-generated binding JAR

2.1 alternative: Package compiled classes into a jar and run with classpath (alternative)
jar cf java-tests.jar -C java-tests .
java -Djava.library.path=mdv_bindings/mdv/java -cp mdv_bindings/mdv/java/mdv4j.jar:java-tests.jar CrudTest
Create an executable JAR (manifest) — if you prefer a single jar
printf "Main-Class: CrudTest\nClass-Path: mdv4j.jar\n" > manifest.mf
jar cfm CrudTest.jar manifest.mf -C java-tests .
Ensure mdv4j.jar is alongside CrudTest.jar (or update Class-Path to point to the correct path).
Run:
java -Djava.library.path=mdv_bindings/mdv/java -jar CrudTest.jar

3. Quick diagnostics (if things fail)

Confirm JAR contents (do the mdv classes exist inside the SWIG jar?):
jar tf mdv_bindings/mdv/java/mdv4j.jar | grep '^mdv/' | sed -n '1,200p'
If javac says "package mdv does not exist":
Use the exact jar filename in -cp (maybe mdv4j-1.0.0.jar instead of mdv4j.jar).

Example:
javac -cp mdv_bindings/mdv/java/mdv4j-1.0.0.jar ../mdv_bindings/mdv/java/tests/CrudTest.java -d java-tests
If JVM throws UnsatisfiedLinkError: no mdv4j in java.library.path:
Confirm libmdv4j.so exists: ls -l mdv_bindings/mdv/java
Ensure JVM arch matches the native lib (java -version and uname -m)
